eZ Oracle extension [EXTENSION_VERSION] INSTALL

Introduction
============

1. What is the eZ Oracle extension?
   --------------------------------

   This extension adds support for the Oracle database to eZ Publish by
   plugging into the database framework. After it is installed you can
   connect to any Oracle database that fits the minimum requirements.

2. License
   -------

   This software is licensed under the [EXTENSION_LICENSE]. The complete
   license agreement is included in the LICENSE file. For more information
   or questions please contact info@ez.no

3. Requirements
   ------------

   The following requirements exists for using eZ Oracle extension:

   o  eZ Publish version:

      Make sure you use eZ Publish version [EXTENSION_PUBLISH_VERSION] or higher
      together with this extension.
      It is generally recommended to always use the latest stable version of
      eZ Publish when running on Oracle.

   o  Oracle version:

      Make sure you have Oracle 9, 10 or 11 (client and server).

      If the Oracle server version is less than 9.2, setting the environment
      variable NLS_LANG is mandatory to indicate that php expects to receive
      data from the database in the UTF8 character set.

   o  PHP with Oracle support (oci8 extension):

      Make sure your PHP installation has compiled in support for Oracle. To
      compile this you will need the Oracle client libraries and headers.
      The standard windows version of php includes the dll needed for oracle support
      (but the oracle client is still neded).
      To check if Oracle support is enabled, the output of the phpinfo() php
      function includes a section titled "oci8". From the command line run `php -i'

   o  Oracle user with correct privileges:

      Make sure you have a user on the oracle server that can create the
      following elements: session, table, trigger, sequence and procedure.


Getting eZ Publish
==================

You can download a version of eZ Publish from ez.no, you will find the
various versions at:
http://ez.no/ (enterprise versions)
http://share.ez.no/download-develop (community versions)

NOTE: You will require eZ Publish [EXTENSION_PUBLISH_VERSION] or higher

Information on the installation of eZ Publish can be found at:
http://doc.ez.no/eZ-Publish/Technical-manual/


Restarting Apache
=================

   Detailed instructions on the installation and configuration of the oci8
   extension are available at http://www.php.net/manual/en/oci8.setup.php.

   Possibly you will have to restart the Apache webserver to make sure PHP is
   loaded with Oracle support and the needed enviroment variable are in effect.

   $ . oraenv
   $ apachectl restart

   NOTE: Each time you restart the webserver you might need to run the `oraenv'
         command, if not the Oracle extension in PHP will not work properly.


Installing eZ Oracle extension
==============================

1. Copy the package into the `extension' directory in the root of your
   eZ Publish installation.

2. Unpack the files in the distribution.

   $ unzip ezoracle-[EXTENSION_VERSION].zip

3. Enable the extension in eZ Publish. To do this edit site.ini.append.php in the
   folder root_of_ezpublish/settings/override. If this file does not exist, create it.
   Locate (or add) the block [ExtensionSettings] and add the line:

   ActiveExtensions[]=ezoracle

   If you run several sites using only one distribution and only some of the sites should use
   the extension, make the changes in the override file of that siteaccess.
   E.g root_of_ezpublish/settings/siteaccess/news/site.ini.append.php
   But instead of using ActiveExtensions you must add these lines instead:

   [ExtensionSettings]
   ActiveAccessExtensions[]=ezoracle

   Regenerate the extension autoload file, either via GUI (in case the  eZ Publish
   site is already installed ) in the Admin interface, "Setup" tab, "Extensions" page,
   or by executing the ezpgenerateautoloads.php file.


Initializing Oracle database
============================

1. Automated install with ora-initialize.sh
   ----------------------------------------

   1. Start from scratch
      ------------------

      If you want to start with a completely new eZ Publish database, go to the
      eZ Publish installation directory and run the script:

      $ ./extension/ezoracle/bin/shell/ora-initialize.sh

      Warning: when using this method, the tables needed by extensions will not
      be automatically created.

   2. Migrate from an existing database
      ---------------------------------

      To migrate an existing database with its data, you first need to generate
      the schema file and the data file with the following commands (launched
      from the eZ Publish installation directory):

      $ ./bin/php/ezsqldumpschema.php --output-types=schema --format=generic \
        --output-array --type=mysql \
        --host=<host> --user=<user> --password=<password> \
        <database> path/to/schema.dba

      $ ./bin/php/ezsqldumpschema.php --output-types=data --format=generic \
        --output-array --type=mysql \
        --host=<host> --user=<user> --password=<password> \
        <database> path/to/data.dba

      Then, you can run the ora-initialize.sh script with the previously
      generated files in parameter.

      $ ./extension/ezoracle/bin/shell/ora-initialize.sh -s path/to/schema.dba \
        -d path/to/data.dba


   The script will check your system against the requirements. If something
   is not as it should be it will give you information on the problem.
   The script will ask for some information in some steps, the steps are:

   o  Testing connection to Oracle server

      This step will check if the Oracle server can be connected to using PHP.
      Running this step is recommended since it will give information on what
      can be done if it fails.

      The following information will be asked for:

      Username: Which user to connect with
      Password: The password for the user
      Instance: The name of the oracle instance, this must match the instance
                configured on the client. If you are unsure about this you can
                use the one set in the ORACLE_SID environment variable.

   o  Create a new user

      This step can create a new user with the required privileges for you.
      This is the user that will be the owner of the eZ Publish objects (schema).
      If you already have a user with the required privileges you can skip
      this step.

   o  Connecting to Oracle server

      This step will read in information which will be used to connect to the
      server in later steps. The specified user must have the required
      privileges.
      Note: This step will be skipped if a user is created in the step above.

   Once the script has all necessary information it will connect to the server
   and initialize the Oracle database.

   o  Creating md5_digest and bitor functions

      The script still creates them, for SQL of your own that calls them; the
      driver itself no longer needs them (see the manual install below).
      Your user will require `CREATE PROCEDURE' privilege for this.

   o  Cleaning up existing elements in database

      If eZ Publish has been previously installed it will remove any elements
      left.

   o  Creating the database schema and inserting data

      The schema and data will be taken from the eZ Publish installation files
      or from the command line and the database will be initialized with them.

2. Manual install
   --------------

   Suppose you have a running Oracle instance named "orcl" which you can access
   as user "scott" with password "tiger", and you want to run eZ Publish on it.
   Here are several steps to achieve this goal:

   o  Environment variables

      Set all the needed environment variables by eg. running `oraenv'.

      $ . oraenv

      (you may need to consult with your DBA to know which SID to enter)

   o  Setup a database user:

      You must make sure you have a user with the correct privileges

# Here user "system" (DBA) has password "sys".
#################################################################
$ cat <<EOF | sqlplus system/sys@orcl
CREATE USER scott IDENTIFIED BY tiger QUOTA UNLIMITED ON SYSTEM;
GRANT CREATE    SESSION   TO scott;
GRANT CREATE    TABLE     TO scott;
GRANT CREATE    TRIGGER   TO scott;
GRANT CREATE    SEQUENCE  TO scott;
GRANT CREATE    PROCEDURE TO scott;
EOF
#################################################################

      You will see "ORA-01920: user name 'SCOTT' conflicts with another user
      or role name" if user scott already exists.
      If the Oracle server is version 10g or later, new users might be created
      with a default tablespace which is not SYSTEM; you should alter the above sql
      command accordingly to grant unlimited quota on the correct tablespace
      (and possibly specify a customised default tablespace for the user)

   o  Create md5_digest and bitor functions (optional)

      The driver no longer needs them: it computes MD5 with STANDARD_HASH
      (Oracle 12.1 and later) and bitwise OR with BITAND. Create them only
      for SQL of your own that calls md5_digest() or bitor(). Your user will
      require `CREATE PROCEDURE' privilege for this.
      $ sqlplus scott/tiger@orcl < extension/ezoracle/sql/md5_digest.sql
      $ sqlplus scott/tiger@orcl < extension/ezoracle/sql/bitor.sql

   o  Initialize the schema

     The schema must now be created with all the tables, sequences and
     triggers for eZ Publish. Then the schema must be filled with data.
     Here you have two choices:

     1. Start from scratch
        ------------------

        You can start from scratch by using the schema bundled with the
        eZ Publish installation, and filling it with the basic data.

        o  Import the schema
           $ ./bin/php/ezsqlinsertschema.php --type=oracle \
               --user=<user> --password=<password> \
               --insert-types=schema \
               share/db_schema.dba <instance>

        o  Import the data
           $ ./bin/php/ezsqlinsertschema.php --type=oracle \
               --user=<user> --password=<password> \
               --insert-types=data \
               --schema-file=db_data.dba \
               share/db_data.dba <instance>

     2. Migrate from existing database
        ------------------------------

        If you want/need to migrate an existing database, you can achieve
        this with the native eZ Publish tools. Don't forget to add the --siteaccess
        parameter if your default siteaccess isn't sufficient information::

        o  Dump your existing database to the local database.dba file::

           $ ./bin/php/ezsqldumpschema.php --output-types=all --format=generic \
             --output-array --type=mysql \
             --host=<host> --user=<user> --password=<password> \
             <database> database.dba

        o  Insert the dumped data to your oracle database::

           $ ./bin/php/ezsqlinsertschema.php --type=oracle \
               --user=<user> --password=<password> \
               --insert-types=all \
               --schema-file=database.dba database.dba <instance>

Configuring Exponential
=======================

1. Installing a new site on Oracle
   -------------------------------

   With the extension in extension/ezoracle and oci8 loaded, the installers
   offer Oracle (Exponential 6.0.15 or later) and activate the extension for
   the new site:

   $ EXP_INSTALL_DB_PASSWORD=<password> ./bin/php/console exp:install \
         --db=oracle --db-host=127.0.0.1 --db-port=1521 --db-name=FREEPDB1 \
         --db-user=<user>

   --db-name is the service name (host:port/service is built from it); a full
   connect string or @<tns-alias> is used as it is. For the kickstarter set
   [database_choice] Type=oracle and Database=<connect string> in
   [database_init] and [site_details] (see kickstart.ini-dist).

2. Changing the database handler of an existing site
   -------------------------------------------------

   Edit settings/override/site.ini.append.php:

##################################
[DatabaseSettings]
DatabaseImplementation=ezoracle
User=<user>
Password=<password>
# the Oracle connect string: Easy Connect host:port/service_name,
# Easy Connect Plus tcp://host:port/service?connect_timeout=5, a TNS alias,
# or a full (DESCRIPTION=...) descriptor. Server and Port are not used.
Database=127.0.0.1:1521/FREEPDB1
Charset=utf-8

[ExtensionSettings]
ActiveExtensions[]=ezoracle
##################################

   then regenerate the autoloads: php bin/php/ezpgenerateautoloads.php -e

3. Environment
   -----------

   ORACLE_HOME is not needed with the Instant Client. NLS_LANG is not needed:
   the driver connects in AL32UTF8 (the charset of Charset=utf-8, and the
   default). LD_LIBRARY_PATH is needed only for a client outside the system
   library path; set it (and TNS_ADMIN, if TNS aliases are used) in the PHP-FPM
   pool (env[...]) and in Velocity's environment as well.

   oci8 settings that only php.ini can set: oci8.statement_cache_size
   (statement cache, default 20), oci8.default_prefetch, oci8.prefetch_lob_size,
   oci8.ping_interval, oci8.persistent_timeout and oci8.max_persistent.

Settings reference
==================

   Every feature below is off by default. The driver's own settings are in
   extension/ezoracle/settings/ezoracle.ini; override them in
   settings/override/ezoracle.ini.append.php (or per siteaccess). Two options
   of Oracle's semantics are in site.ini [DatabaseSettings]. After changing INI
   files clear the INI cache: php bin/php/ezcache.php --clear-tag=ini

1. Empty string and NULL (site.ini [DatabaseSettings])
   ----------------------------------------------------

   Oracle stores '' as NULL. The schema handler makes text columns nullable
   on Oracle, so the empty strings Exponential writes are accepted.

   OracleEmptyStringForNull=disabled   (default) NULL comes back as Oracle has it
   OracleEmptyStringForNull=enabled    NULL in CHAR/VARCHAR2/CLOB columns comes
                                       back as '', as the other databases return
                                       the empty strings that were written; real
                                       NULLs in text columns then read as '' too.
                                       Numeric columns always keep NULL.

2. Case-insensitive comparisons (site.ini [DatabaseSettings])
   ----------------------------------------------------------

   Oracle compares strings binary and case sensitive; MySQL's *_ci collations
   do not (logins, identifiers, sorted lists).

   OracleCaseInsensitive=enabled
   OracleCaseInsensitiveSort=BINARY_CI     (BINARY_AI ignores accents too, or any
                                            linguistic sort name, e.g. GERMAN_CI)

   Every session then runs ALTER SESSION SET NLS_COMP=LINGUISTIC
   NLS_SORT=<sort>. Index implications: a plain index is no longer used for =,
   LIKE, IN or ORDER BY on a string column; queries on such columns scan the
   table unless there is a linguistic index on NLSSORT( column,
   'NLS_SORT=<sort>' ). Create them for the columns Exponential compares with

   $ ./bin/php/console ext:ezoracle:ci-indexes --create -s <siteaccess>

   (the columns are ezoracle.ini [CaseInsensitiveSettings] Columns[]; --drop
   removes them, without an option they are listed). Unique constraints stay
   binary: two logins that differ only in case can still both exist.

3. Connection ([ConnectionSettings] in ezoracle.ini)
   -------------------------------------------------

   Persistent=inherit|enabled|disabled   oci_pconnect; inherit follows site.ini
                                         UsePersistentConnection
   DRCP=enabled                          pooled servers: :POOLED is appended to an
                                         Easy Connect string, SERVER=POOLED to a
                                         built descriptor; for a TNS alias put
                                         (SERVER=POOLED) into tnsnames.ora. The pool
                                         must be started in the database:
                                         EXECUTE DBMS_CONNECTION_POOL.START_POOL
   ConnectionClass=EXPONENTIAL           oci8.connection_class for DRCP
   ConnectString=                        instead of site.ini Database
   TnsAdmin=/etc/oracle                  directory of tnsnames.ora/sqlnet.ora. The
                                         client reads it once per process: the
                                         environment (FPM pool, Velocity) is the
                                         surer place
   Edition=                              edition-based redefinition

   Failover and load balancing over several listeners (a descriptor is built
   when Hosts[] and ServiceName are set and ConnectString is empty):

##################################
[ConnectionSettings]
Hosts[]
Hosts[]=db1.example.com:1521
Hosts[]=db2.example.com:1521
ServiceName=EXPPRD
Failover=enabled
LoadBalance=disabled
ConnectTimeout=5
##################################

   Retries and lost connections:

   RetryCount=3, RetryDelay=1, RetryBackoff=2
                  3 more attempts after 1, 2 and 4 seconds (site.ini
                  ConnectRetries when RetryCount is not set)
   ReconnectErrors[]=3113 ...
                  the ORA- numbers that mean a lost or unreachable connection
                  (the shipped list covers ORA-00028, 01012, 01033, 01034, 01089,
                  03113, 03114, 03135, 12153, 12170, 12514, 12528, 12537, 12541,
                  12543, 12545, 12547, 12570, 25408). On one of them outside a
                  transaction the driver reconnects; a read (SELECT, WITH) is run
                  again on the new connection (RetryReads=enabled), a write is
                  reported, as it may have been carried out before the connection
                  broke. Inside a transaction the error is raised as before: the
                  transaction's work is lost with the session.
   CallTimeout=30000
                  milliseconds one round trip may take (oci_set_call_timeout,
                  client 18c+); longer calls end with ORA-03156. Data Pump runs of
                  ext:ezoracle:datapump are exempt.
   KeepAliveInterval=60
                  for long running processes: after that many idle seconds the
                  next statement is preceded by a round trip, and a dead
                  connection (database restarted, firewall timeout) is replaced
                  first. Recommended for Velocity workers and cronjobs.

4. Performance ([PerformanceSettings])
   -----------------------------------

   Prefetch=500        rows per round trip for queries (oci_set_prefetch)
   LobPrefetch=16384   LOB bytes sent with the row (oci_set_prefetch_lob)

   Limits and offsets are applied by the database (OFFSET/FETCH, 12.1+).

5. Tracing ([TraceSettings])
   -------------------------

##################################
[TraceSettings]
ClientIdentifier=%siteaccess%:%user_id%
ModuleName=Exponential %siteaccess%
Action=%module%/%view%
ClientInfo=pid %pid%
##################################

   The values appear in V$SESSION (CLIENT_IDENTIFIER, MODULE, ACTION,
   CLIENT_INFO) and in AWR/ASH reports, and ext:ezoracle:report shows them
   next to the top SQL. Patterns: %siteaccess% %module% %view% %user_id%
   %script% %pid%. They are sent with the next round trip when they change.

6. Logging ([LogSettings])
   -----------------------

   SlowQueryThreshold=500      statements taking 500 ms or more go to
                               var/log/<SlowQueryLog> (default oracle-slow.log)
   MaskLiterals=enabled        string literals of the logged SQL become '?';
                               bound values are always logged as their length
   StatementCounts=enabled     statement counts per type (SELECT, INSERT ...) in
                               the debug output's accumulators (with
                               DebugOutput=enabled)

Console commands
================

   ./bin/php/console ext:ezoracle:<command> [-s <siteaccess>] [options]

   Every command has --help, prints one PASS/WARN/FAIL/INFO line per check and
   ends with exit status 1 when one failed. Those that change something take
   --dry-run. V$ and DBA views need SELECT_CATALOG_ROLE; without it the
   affected checks say so.

   health             connection, versions, charset, invalid objects, tablespaces,
                      sessions, blocking locks, recycle bin, sequences, statistics
   gather-stats       DBMS_STATS.GATHER_SCHEMA_STATS ([StatisticsSettings])
   recompile          DBMS_UTILITY.COMPILE_SCHEMA for invalid objects
   purge-recyclebin   PURGE RECYCLEBIN
   sequence-sync      sequences behind their tables' ids; --fix moves them
                      (bin/php/ora-update-seqs.php does it from a login string)
   schema-diff        the database against share/db_schema.dba (--file=<dba>),
                      with the upgrade SQL
   indexes            index report; --rebuild [--index=<name>] [--online];
                      --monitor-on, --monitor-off, --usage
   report             --sizes (tables with index and LOB space), --top-sql (V$SQL)
   datapump           --export=<file.dmp> / --import=<file.dmp>
                      [--table-exists-action=SKIP|APPEND|TRUNCATE|REPLACE]
                      [--remap-schema=FROM:TO] through DBMS_DATAPUMP into the
                      directory object of [DataPumpSettings] Directory (default
                      DATA_PUMP_DIR; the user needs READ, WRITE on it). The same
                      with the server tools: expdp <user>@<db> schemas=<user>
                      directory=DATA_PUMP_DIR dumpfile=<file.dmp>, impdp ...
   ci-indexes         linguistic indexes for OracleCaseInsensitive (--create, --drop)

Cronjobs
========

   The cronjob part "ezoracle" runs the statistics, recycle bin, sequence and
   health jobs; "ezoraclehealth" the health check alone. Each job does nothing
   until it is enabled:

##################################
[CronjobSettings]
GatherStatistics=enabled
HealthMonitor=enabled
HealthMailReceivers[]=dba@example.com
PurgeRecycleBin=enabled
SequenceCheck=enabled
SequenceFix=disabled
##################################

   in settings/override/ezoracle.ini.append.php, and e.g. in crontab:

   15 3 * * *  cd /path/to/exponential && php runcronjobs.php -q -s <admin siteaccess> ezoracle
   0 * * * *   cd /path/to/exponential && php runcronjobs.php -q -s <admin siteaccess> ezoraclehealth

   Reports go to var/log/ezoracle-<job>.log; the health monitor mails its
   report to HealthMailReceivers[] when a check warns or fails.

